$Id: README.internals,v 1.9 2011/05/27 11:45:24 drmiller Exp $

Communication between the Front End and the simulator is via two pipes,
one for each direction. All communication is in units of uint16_t.

The simulator reads and interprets the following (binary) codes on
it's input pipe:

00000000kkkkkkkk
	Keyboard code 'k' was entered. E.g. the user presses "Search" and
	the code 0x0080 is sent to the simulator on the pipe.

0000000100000kkk
	"Special" key: PRIME, SET P.C., VERIFY PROG, RECORD PROG, and SHIFT
	variations. 'k' is the microcode address to jump to:
	0 = PRIME
	1 = VERIFY PROG
	2 = SET P.C.
	3 = RECORD PROG
	4 = S.M.
	5 = B.S.
	6 = INS
	7 = DEL

000000100000mmmm
	Mode register 0 changed, new value is 'mm'.
	Bit meanings, 1/(0):
	b0 = Sc/(Fl)
	b1 = List or Learn-and-Print
	b2 = Learn or Learn-and-Print
	b3 = Step (implied pulse)

000000110000mmmm
	Mode register 1 changed, new value is 'mm'.
	Bit meanings, 1/(0):
	b0 = Rad/(Deg)
	b1 = Printer On/(Off)
	b2 = n.c.
	b3 = n.c.

00001100tttttttt
	Tape read data, byte 'tt'.
0000111000000000
	Tape read EOF. (END PROG)

00110000xxxxxxxx
	Input data for "Device 2", Model 630 "Fixed/Removable Disk".

01000000xxxxxxxx
	Input data for "Device 4", multiple possible devices. TBD

01010000xxxxxxxx
	Input data for "Device 5", multiple possible devices. TBD

0nnn0001xxxxxxxx
	ACK for I/O devices N=1..5, in response to simulator sending
	a byte with the same N. 'x' is (should be) ignored.

10000001xxxxxxxx
	Prepare for ROM download. 'x' is (should be) ignored.
	Simulator ACKs. ROM begins loading at 0xfff and procedes
	towards 0x000.

10000000xxxxxxxx
	Byte for ROM, sent in-bulk after ACK for prepare.
	Simulator waits for each byte.

11111111xxxxxxxx
	End of ROM download. 'x' is (should be) ignored.


The simulator sends the following (binary) codes on it's output pipe:

00000eeessssgggg
	Display refresh data. 's' is digit select, 'g' is digit code,
	'e' is error/blanking code, bits 0-2.
	Note, traffic is "optimized" such that once a couple copies have
	been sent, the simulator stops sending until a key is pressed.
	Bits 0 and 1 of 'e' indicate the current state of the Mach Err and
	Prog Err lights, respectively. Either one of those results in
	a flickering display. Bit 2 of 'e' indicates display is blanked,
	causing 's' and 'g' to be ignored.

0000100sssssgggg
	Printer data, 's' is column (overflows into lo bit of '8'),
	'g' is drum code (character to print). 's' value > 19 means
	(print and) feed. Note, column/drum values are not sent in
	a contiguous pattern. All columns for drum '0' (if any) are
	sent at once, then all columns for drum '1', etc. A 'feed'
	command that does not follow column/drum data prints a blank
	line.

00001100tttttttt
	Tape "record" data, byte 'tt'.
0000110100000000
	Tape On for read.
0000110100000001
	Request front-end to send 1 byte from tape.
0000111100000000
	Tape On for write
0000111000000000
	Tape Off (FE should stop sending, if read).

0001000000rrrrrr
	Print data for "Device 1", Model 601/602/611 "Output Writer".
	'r' is a 6-bit (2+4) "tilt/rotate" (or special-function) code for the printer.

001c0000xxxxxxxx
	Output data for "Device 2", Model 630 "Fixed/Removable Disk".
	'c' is command/data bit (1 = data).

01000000xxxxxxxx
	Output data for "Device 4", multiple possible devices.

01010000xxxxxxxx
	Output data for "Device 5", multiple possible devices.

0nnn0001xxxxxxxx
	ACK for I/O devices N=1..5, in response to GUI sending
	a byte with the same N. 'x' is (should be) ignored.

01111111xxxxxxxx
	Reset I/O. 'x' is (should be) ignored.

10000001xxxxxxxx
	ACK for ROM download. 'x' is (should be) ignored. GUI may now
	send bulk ROM image.
